exports[`capability card > is byte-identical across runs and carries names, types, descriptions, relationships, enum meanings, grains and the declared date range 1`] = `
"# Capability card
Schema of the bound project: names, types, descriptions, relationships, cubes, enum meanings, declared time grains and, when the project has them, its knowledge rules (authored business definitions, quoted as written). It carries no sample values, row counts, model or view SQL definitions or saved queries; ask the data oracle for values.
project: card_demo (duckdb)

## Models
- customers [pk: id] — One row per customer account.
  - id: INTEGER (not null) — Unique customer id.
  - name: VARCHAR — Customer display name.
  - tier: VARCHAR — [enum] Commercial tier; see enum_values.
  - status: VARCHAR
- order_lines
  - order_id: INTEGER
  - quantity: INTEGER
- orders [pk: id] — One row per order, amount in USD.
  - id: INTEGER (not null)
  - customer_id: INTEGER (relationship: orders_customer)
  - order_date: DATE — Order date (UTC).
  - amount: DOUBLE
  - amount_cents: BIGINT (calculated)

## Relationships
- orders_customer: orders customers MANY_TO_ONE on orders.customer_id = customers.id

## Cubes
A cube is a semantic definition (a base model plus measures and dimensions), not a table: never query a cube by name in SQL. Query its base model and compute a measure from that model's columns.
- order_metrics (cube, not a table; base model: orders) — Revenue and order counts.
  measures: total_revenue DOUBLE — Sum of order amounts in USD.; order_count BIGINT
  dimensions: customer_id INTEGER
  time dimensions: order_date DATE [grains: day/month/quarter/year; range: 2023-01-01..2024-12-31]

## Views
- customer_orders — Orders joined to their customer.

## Enum meanings
- customers.tier: ENT = enterprise; SMB = small business
- customers.status: A = active; C = churned

## Knowledge rules

Business rules of the bound project (canonical metric definitions, default filters, units, enum meanings). Follow them when choosing tables, filters and metric definitions.

### knowledge/rules/general.md
## Currency
All amounts are USD.

### knowledge/rules/net-revenue.md
## Net revenue
Net revenue = SUM(orders.amount_cents + orders.fee_cents) / 100 per order, never the gross amount."
`;
